al, and the ledger was real, and that was enough."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Squeeze', 'text': "The dame vanished. Of course she did. Ray Donovan found her scarf in the office, red as a warning, and underneath it a note: 'Forgive me. They have my brother.' So that was the price. Everyone has one.\n\nRuby's club was next — a fire in the kitchen, a message in the smoke. Ray Donovan found her unharmed and furious, and that fury was worth ten guns. 'End this,' she said. Ray Donovan intended to.\n\nLou the bartender showed up bloody and grinning. 'They hit like accountants,' Lou the bartender said. Ray Donovan felt the cold anger settle in, the kind that doesn't shout, just aims."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Confrontation', 'text': "'You're finished,' Ray Donovan said. Mr. Big smiled the smile of someone who has bought judges. 'Am I?' Then Lou the bartender opened the door with the district attorney, and Ruby, and the dame's brother, alive, and the smile died.\n\nThe dame got her brother back and Ray Donovan got the rest of the fee, which was more than the insulting original. She cried. Ray Donovan didn't buy the tears but didn't charge for the shoulder, either.\n\nIt went down the way it always goes: shouting, threats, a gun that Ray Donovan was faster than. When the police took Mr. Big out, the rain-slicked city seemed to exhale, like a drunk finally telling the truth."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Last Call', 'text': "The dame left town on the morning train, brother in tow, free and clean. She left Ray Donovan a note: 'You were the only honest thing in it.' Ray Donovan kept the note. Detectives keep everything.\n\nThe phone rang. Ray Donovan picked it up. 'Ray Donovan Investigations,' Ray Donovan said, and listened, and smiled the thin smile of someone who knows exactly how much trouble costs. 'redemption costs extra. I'll take the case.'\n\nthe rain-slicked city kept being the rain-slicked city: rain, neon, lies, and now and then — now and then — a little justice, served neat, no ice. Ray Donovan could live with that. Ray Donovan had lived with worse."}]}

